-### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Memory Overflow**: Naive partitioning can lead to unbalanced node distribution. The BFS method ensures balanced distribution to mitigate this [S1].
-*   **Boundary Artifacts**: Combining local Gaussians naively causes floating artifacts or holes. The progressive rendering strategy with global supplementation resolves this [S1].
-*   **Textureless Regions**: Standard 3DGS struggles with geometry here. The multi-view constraint using NCC addresses this [S1].
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **Memory Overflow:** Unbalanced node distribution in segmentation can lead to memory overflow [S1]. The BFS method is chosen specifically to prevent this [S1].
+*   **Boundary Artifacts:** Naively combining local Gaussians can cause floating artifacts or holes at region boundaries [S1]. The progressive rendering strategy with matching scores mitigates this [S1].
+*   **Textureless Regions:** Standard 3DGS struggles with geometry in textureless areas; the multi-view constraint is required to address this [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-
-### Time and Memory Complexity
-*   **Memory**: Parallel training of local Gaussians reduces peak GPU memory usage compared to training a single global model. Global training uses downsampled data to further limit memory [S1].
-*   **Time**: Graph construction and segmentation add preprocessing overhead. However, parallel local training accelerates convergence. Progressive rendering adds computational cost per pixel during novel view synthesis compared to standard splatting [S1].
-
-### Measured Performance
-*   Evaluated on large-scale aerial (Mill-19, UrbanScene3D) and ground-level (SmallCity, Campus, Road, General) datasets with thousands of images [S1].
-*   Maintains real-time performance across vastly different scales [S1].
-
-### Strengths and Limitations
-*   **Strengths**: Handles large-scale scenes with varying distances; improves texture and geometry in textureless regions; eliminates boundary artifacts in novel views [S1].
-*   **Limitations**: Requires feature matching for graph construction; relies on specific densification protocols for multi-scale handling [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Training is parallelized across regions, reducing per-region memory and potentially time [S1]. Novel view rendering involves sorting local Gaussians and progressive blending, adding overhead compared to standard alpha-blending [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** By partitioning the scene and using a coarse global representation for novel views, memory usage is controlled. Global training avoids densification to prevent excessive consumption [S1].
+*   **Performance:** Evaluated on large-scale aerial (up to 3,019 images) and ground datasets (e.g., SmallCity, Campus) [S1]. Maintains real-time performance across vastly different scales [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Handles arbitrary large-scale scenes; improves texture details and geometry in textureless regions; avoids boundary artifacts in novel views.
+*   **Limitations:** Requires feature matching for graph construction; performance depends on the quality of the graph segmentation [S1].
 
 ## Background
-
-### Origin
-TraGraph-GS was proposed to address the limitations of existing methods in rendering arbitrary large-scale scenes, specifically tackling issues with boundary artifacts and textureless regions [S1].
-
-### History and Development
-The method builds upon 3D Gaussian Splatting [11] and addresses issues found in prior large-scale methods like VastGaussian [14] and others [2, 1] by introducing a trajectory graph-based partitioning and progressive rendering strategy [S1].
-
-### Usage
-Used for high-quality rendering of large-scale scenes, including aerial imagery and ground-level urban environments [S1].
+TraGraph-GS was proposed to address the limitations of existing methods in rendering large-scale scenes, such as memory overflow and boundary artifacts. It builds on the principles of 3D Gaussian Splatting [11] and extends them with graph-based partitioning and regularization constraints. The method was introduced in a paper published in IEEE Transactions on Pattern Analysis and Machine Intelligence in 2026 [S1].
 
